1 17592186045417 true] [17592186435332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435328 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435332 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435332 1077 "selection" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435332 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435317 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435332 1079 #uuid "1f879c13-dbbc-55d7-b911-a1fc2b3863d9" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435333 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435320 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435333 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435322 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435333 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435323 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435333 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435326 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435333 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435327 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435333 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435333 1077 "selection" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435333 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435327 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435333 1079 #uuid "984d6c5b-f37c-575f-adde-0cd67c2d7720" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435331 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435334 1069 "q-162590" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435334 1071 "![](images/5f/5fa49c55e5741ccf46a2c77dd5e1770a8e5e7b89a29641af44e42eecbbb82f17.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(2,-1)$ and $B(-1,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(2x+y)\\,i+(-2y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(-3t+2)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435334 1072 #uuid "21126858-0865-5434-a374-dbf17e23e496" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435334 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)=2-2=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6bb6e38f83006fa9874ca0020e4eb016ea8eed7fec79596e984b94a0f3853d75.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(-3t+2)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2x+y=2(-3t+2)+(2t-1)=-4t+3, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-2y=-2(2t-1)=-4t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t+2)=-3, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t+3)\\cdot (2)-(-4t+2)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-8t+6)+(6-12t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12-20t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[12t-10{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-10) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435333 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435335 1069 "q-162558" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435335 1071 "Given the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2x{y}^{2}\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F$\n\nIII. There exists a close curve $C$ such that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\ne 0$" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435335 1072 #uuid "568651db-e544-5f07-84be-3a27fc735107" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435335 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2x{y}^{2}\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+4xy=2x(1+2y)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Indeed, they follow from the fact that $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435332 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435336 1069 "q-162554" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435336 1071 "![](images/62/62946451fd0305439366bdc22db84f8a5823481082e8ac39858369fbc582676d.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435336 1072 #uuid "dd61f71d-b16d-557d-a304-ee717f93d77f" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186435336 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is *not* a source-free vector field. \n\nSince $F$ is not source-free, we do not have enough information to determine the flux of $F$ through $C.$" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-08735882-9207-5309-a7e8-afc080bb5d20" 17592186435316 "answer-0d718fae-7a62-50b9-a0aa-cf9733addd96" 17592186435317 "answer-0de951e4-d86b-5202-9ac2-026b4bcf6c5f" 17592186435318 "answer-0e2fa68f-c7f0-5f8d-9ba5-b91308434c7a" 17592186435319 "answer-12950e32-6502-5362-9826-8bea8d1040b4" 17592186435320 "answer-1670c8fd-d03b-5200-a07e-b492d22de093" 17592186435321 "answer-2952bfeb-9ffe-59c6-8382-80bb73507c7c" 17592186435322 "answer-4986aec7-0bc6-58da-840b-ace2ec7bef03" 17592186435323 "answer-511b422f-6da0-5ba2-afb2-87e7a51ca4a1" 17592186435324 "answer-64e7b7f1-9e0e-5777-8160-d11edf79c8d5" 17592186435325 "answer-8ef61b54-efe4-55bd-bf3c-33b3e7236ba5" 17592186435326 "answer-9f404acf-6d24-59ae-b9e4-103a63b7dfe2" 17592186435327 "answer-a29a253a-123d-5aee-b6ed-f838b5093d69" 17592186435328 "answer-cc9da38b-fae8-51bd-ae43-39acaa1e089a" 17592186435329 "answer-df4b2841-2e6a-5e02-b6d1-1b6fce984f1b" 17592186435330 "answer-field-19bee987-1ec3-5c8c-ac5a-92a635a303b9" 17592186435331 "answer-field-1f879c13-dbbc-55d7-b911-a1fc2b3863d9" 17592186435332 "answer-field-984d6c5b-f37c-575f-adde-0cd67c2d7720" 17592186435333 "question-21126858-0865-5434-a374-dbf17e23e496" 17592186435334 "question-568651db-e544-5f07-84be-3a27fc735107" 17592186435335 "question-dd61f71d-b16d-557d-a304-ee717f93d77f" 17592186435336} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
